%description:

RFC 5681 -- three duplicate acknowledgments repair a loss without the timer.

Checks RFC5681-FR-1 (should), RFC5681-FR-2 (must), RFC5681-FR-3 (must) and RFC5681-FR-5
(must); covers RFC5681-ACK-2.

RFC 5681 section 3.2: three duplicate acknowledgments indicate a loss. The sender
retransmits at once, sets the threshold by the same formula a timeout uses, sets the window
to the threshold plus three segments, and puts the window back to the threshold when the
repair is acknowledged.

The five statements are one episode and the steps below follow it in order. The third
duplicate is the trigger, so everything after it is the response, and anchoring there keeps
the steps away from the threshold publication the model makes early, before any loss.

**One segment is removed, not a window.** This is the opposite of the timeout check: here the
later segments must get through, because they are what makes host B answer with duplicate
acknowledgments. `nth` removes exactly one; `fromNth` would remove the rest with it and the
timer would be the only thing left to detect the loss.

**The window is judged by what host A sends.** RFC 5681 describes the window as a variable
that is inflated: the threshold plus three segments at the third duplicate, and one segment
more for each further duplicate. The model keeps the window of Linux instead. Without SACK,
each duplicate acknowledgment takes one segment out of the data the model counts in flight
(an inferred SACK), and the published `cwnd` stays at the threshold. The two forms give the
same sending room, but the published value is not the RFC's variable. So the check reads the
rule where it acts: a guard forbids any segment of new data that host A sends in recovery and
that takes FlightSize past the threshold plus 3 + k segments, with k the further duplicates.
Two observers keep FlightSize and k from the segments and the acknowledgments.

**The scenario runs the RFC 5681 algorithm.** With SACK, the recovery of RFC 6675 replaces
steps 3 to 5. The ini selects Reno without SACK, and says why.

**The model conforms.** The relay removes exactly one frame, host B repeats its
acknowledgment, and host A repairs the loss on the third duplicate. The threshold falls, the
new data that host A sends in recovery stays within the inflated window, and the repair
leaves well inside one second. The guard was verified to judge real sends: with a bound one
segment lower it fires at t=0.2017, where host A sends new data at the bound.

Observation 4 of the check, that the window returns to the threshold when the repair is
acknowledged, is not asserted here. The model publishes the window again at that point, but
telling that publication apart from the growth that follows it needs the acknowledgment of
new data as an anchor, and that anchor is the next check's subject. It is recorded as a
sharpening candidate rather than claimed.

**The retransmission carries a second bound.** It must arrive well before the retransmission
timer would have fired, or a slow timer and fast retransmit look the same. The timer is three
seconds here, and the step allows one.

%includes:
#include <algorithm>
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "PacketField.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// What host A has sent and what host B has acknowledged, kept by two observers that run
// beside the ordered steps. The engine offers every event to the running guards first and
// to the ordered step after them, so a step reads values that already include its own event.
static long smss = 0;             // the largest data length of host A's segments: SMSS
static long highestSent = -1;     // one past the highest octet host A has sent
static bool lastSendWasNew = false; // the last data segment took highestSent further: new data
static long cumulativeAck = -1;   // the highest acknowledgment host A has received
static int duplicates = 0;        // consecutive duplicates of cumulativeAck
static bool inRecovery = false;   // from the third duplicate to the next acknowledgment of new data
static int furtherDuplicates = 0; // duplicates after the third, in recovery

static long fieldOf(const Packet *packet, const char *path)
{
    try {
        return (long)evalPacketField(packet, path).intValue();
    }
    catch (const std::exception&) {
        return -1;
    }
}

// At host A's TCP a segment starts with its TCP header, so the data is what follows it.
static long dataLengthOf(const Packet *packet)
{
    return (long)(packet->getByteLength() - evalPacketField(packet, "tcp.headerLength").doubleValueInUnit("B"));
}

static bool dataSegment(const MatchContext& context)
{
    return context.event.packet != nullptr && dataLengthOf(context.event.packet) > 0;
}

// Observer: every data segment host A sends. Never a match.
static bool recordSent(const MatchContext& context)
{
    const Packet *packet = context.event.packet;
    if (packet == nullptr || fieldOf(packet, "tcp.destPort") != 1000)
        return false;
    long length = dataLengthOf(packet);
    if (length <= 0)
        return false;
    smss = std::max(smss, length);
    long end = fieldOf(packet, "tcp.sequenceNo") + length;
    lastSendWasNew = end > highestSent;
    highestSent = std::max(highestSent, end);
    return false;
}

// Observer: every acknowledgment host A receives. RFC 5681 section 2 counts an
// acknowledgment as a duplicate when it carries no data and repeats the highest
// acknowledgment number; the third one starts fast retransmit, and the next acknowledgment
// of new data ends the recovery. Never a match.
static bool recordAck(const MatchContext& context)
{
    const Packet *packet = context.event.packet;
    if (packet == nullptr || fieldOf(packet, "tcp.srcPort") != 1000)
        return false;
    long ack = fieldOf(packet, "tcp.ackNo");
    if (ack < 0)
        return false;
    if (ack > cumulativeAck) {
        cumulativeAck = ack;
        duplicates = 0;
        inRecovery = false;
    }
    else if (ack == cumulativeAck && dataLengthOf(packet) == 0) {
        duplicates++;
        if (duplicates == 3) {
            inRecovery = true;
            furtherDuplicates = 0;
        }
        else if (inRecovery)
            furtherDuplicates++;
    }
    return false;
}

// True on the third duplicate acknowledgment, which RFC 5681 section 3.2 names as the trigger.
static bool thirdDuplicateAcknowledgment(const MatchContext&)
{
    return inRecovery && duplicates == 3;
}

// RFC 5681 section 3.2, steps 3 to 5: in recovery the window is the threshold plus three
// segments, plus one more segment for each further duplicate, and host A sends previously
// unsent data only while FlightSize stays within it. True when a segment of new data that
// host A sends in recovery takes FlightSize past that bound: the violation the guard forbids.
// The retransmission of step 3 is not new data, and the RFC requires it at once.
static bool beyondTheInflatedWindow(const MatchContext& context)
{
    if (!inRecovery || !dataSegment(context) || !lastSendWasNew)
        return false;
    double threshold = context.get("threshold").doubleValue();
    double flightSize = (double)(highestSent - cumulativeAck);
    return flightSize > threshold + (3 + furtherDuplicates) * (double)smss;
}

// The repair must come well before the retransmission timer.
static bool wellBeforeTheTimer(const MatchContext& context)
{
    double lostAt = context.get("lostAt").doubleValue();
    return context.event.time.dbl() - lostAt < 1.0;
}

static cValue eventTime(const PacketEvent& event) { return cValue(event.time.dbl()); }
static cValue eventValue(const PacketEvent& event) { return cValue(event.value); }

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc5681_fast_retransmit")
        // Remove exactly one segment from the middle of a window. Every later segment gets
        // through, and each one makes host B repeat the same acknowledgment.
        .intercept(tap("relay")
                       .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                       .minBytes(100).nth(20)
                       .drop().describe("one data segment in the middle of a window"))
        .meanwhile(never(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                             .filterEvent(recordSent)
                             .describe("every data segment host A sends")
                             .within(20.0)))
        .meanwhile(never(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                             .filterEvent(recordAck)
                             .describe("every acknowledgment host A receives")
                             .within(20.0)))
        // The segment that is removed. Remember where it starts and when it left.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment).nth(20)
                  .capture("lostSeq", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  .capture("lostAt", eventTime)
                  .describe("the segment the relay removes")
                  .within(20.0))
        // Host B answers each later segment with the same acknowledgment. The third of them
        // is the trigger RFC 5681 names.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterEvent(thirdDuplicateAcknowledgment)
                  .describe("the third duplicate acknowledgment")
                  .within(5.0))
        // The threshold falls. Remember it, so the window can be judged against it.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("ssthresh").first()
                  .capture("threshold", eventValue)
                  .describe("the threshold after the third duplicate")
                  .within(1.0))
        // And the window becomes the threshold plus three segments, and grows by one segment
        // for each further duplicate. The window is what host A may have in flight, so the
        // guard judges every segment host A sends in recovery against it.
        .meanwhile(never(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                             .filterEvent(beyondTheInflatedWindow)
                             .describe("a segment in recovery that takes FlightSize past the threshold plus 3 + k segments")
                             .within(1.0)))
        // The missing segment is sent again at once, well before the timer would fire.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.sequenceNo == {lostSeq} && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment).first()
                  .assertEvent(wellBeforeTheTimer)
                  .describe("the repair, well before the retransmission timer")
                  .within(5.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc5681_fast_retransmit compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;
import inet.protocoltest.PacketTap;

// Two hosts with a relay on the link -- note: NO tester (attached at runtime).
network FastRetransmitNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        relay: PacketTap { @display("p=400,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> relay.a;
        relay.b     <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = FastRetransmitNet
sim-time-limit = 30s
seed-set = 0

**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"
# RFC 5681's fast retransmit and fast recovery are the algorithm of a TCP without SACK. With
# SACK, the recovery of RFC 6675 replaces steps 3 to 5. The check reads RFC 5681, so it runs
# Reno without SACK.
**.tcp.tcpAlgorithmClass = "TcpReno"
**.tcp.sackSupport = false
**.tcp.advertisedWindow = 65535

*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 100000B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = -1s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpSinkApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc5681_fast_retransmit: PASS
